[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} {:answer/id #uuid "3070ff1a-7364-514c-8463-27955639a9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539404} {:answer/id #uuid "c36c1a8a-ce2f-5284-b361-d99daa4298d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539445} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bef1a7-bd82-52c0-a027-9770a4051e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "289.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539446} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f09433-dc8c-51ad-bba5-d5f7876a82dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cc63e8-6dde-540e-9e8a-53412c30e26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd6eb638-b03a-52f5-961d-00f47eebd890" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $290\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=110+25t-20t=5(22+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =25-\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q=25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(22+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|22+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(22+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(22+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q) & =(22+t)^{4}\\cdot 25 \\\\ (22+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(22+t)^{3}Q & =25(22+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4}) & =25(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 25(22+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(22+t)^{4} & =25\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(22+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(22+t)+\\frac{C}{(22+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=55,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =55 \\\\ 5(22+0)+\\frac{C}{(22+0)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ 110+\\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =-55 \\\\ C & =-55(22)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(22+t)-\\frac{55(22)^{4}}{(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =5(22+t)-55{(\\frac{22}{22+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $290\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=290$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =290 \\\\ 110+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 110+5{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =36\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(36) & =5(22+36)-55{(\\frac{22}{22+36})}^{4} \\\\  & =290-55{(\\frac{22}{58})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈288.9\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33657fab-2d53-58a0-98a5-5356a1b0e53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539405} {:answer/id #uuid "481acaa8-6906-5974-ae76-54c5df18a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539412} {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d6b5ed-3b6b-5ab9-afd8-dc8db825fe1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539436} {:answer/id #uuid "ecee97e7-7992-52dc-9300-131e135e7abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6be6c4b-dcbc-5ac0-a692-8e76768f9b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df8520ff-a1e4-578f-8153-7bfeb8954209" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $342gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $117gal$ of nutrient solution with $39lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $45gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $36gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $45\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $117\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $45\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $36\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=117+45t-36t=9(13+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $36\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(36\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =30-\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q=30.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(13+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|13+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(13+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(13+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q) & =(13+t)^{4}\\cdot 30 \\\\ (13+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(13+t)^{3}Q & =30(13+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4}) & =30(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 30(13+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(13+t)^{4} & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(13+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(13+t)+\\frac{C}{(13+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $39\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=39,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =39 \\\\ 6(13+0)+\\frac{C}{(13+0)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ 78+\\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(13)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(13+t)-\\frac{39(13)^{4}}{(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =6(13+t)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $342\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=342$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =342 \\\\ 117+45{t}_{o}-36{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 117+9{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 9{t}_{o} & =225 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =6(13+25)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+25})}^{4} \\\\  & =228-39{(\\frac{13}{38})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈227.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59522728-6ff5-53b4-81e0-6fc27377f260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "139.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539419} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc28afa-a913-5db8-b93f-4620f9101e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539421} {:answer/id #uuid "7393c7e7-e7db-5dae-8dbd-5c124fce7375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539426} {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} {:answer/id #uuid "f63a9d0e-447c-57d4-a11e-5dba82466c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} :answer-field/id #uuid "5063c8db-660b-5ca6-90ed-32587c3a7e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbffe068-df49-5949-a93c-dd6aa67cd689" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $306\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=234+24t-18t=6(39+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(39+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|39+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(39+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(39+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q) & =(39+t)^{3}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (39+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(39+t)^{2}Q & =12(39+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3}) & =12(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 12(39+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(39+t)^{3} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(39+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(39+t)+\\frac{C}{(39+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=78,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =78 \\\\ 3(39+0)+\\frac{C}{(39+0)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ 117+\\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(39)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(39+t)-\\frac{39(39)^{3}}{(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =3(39+t)-39{(\\frac{39}{39+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $306\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=306$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =306 \\\\ 234+24{t}_{o}-18{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 234+6{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =72 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =12\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =3(39+12)-39{(\\frac{39}{39+12})}^{3} \\\\  & =153-39{(\\frac{39}{51})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈135.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf04547-57c3-53c4-840a-33e618532e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "284.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539403} {:answer/id #uuid "5acc0bd1-19cd-5dac-ac79-38ebff3903c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "588.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539420} {:answer/id #uuid "797eee29-2209-55c5-906c-e8f2d054d896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "458.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539427} {:answer/id #uuid "a62cbaf9-65b9-5cb1-860f-98a4438ffabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "273.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} :answer-field/id #uuid "f243c2a4-f8d5-5f1f-93bf-3f804ba469a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff0fe97f-83da-58e7-9a2a-1f41369f6103" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $490\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=315+15t-5t=10(31.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(31.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|31.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(31.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q) & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (31.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(31.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5}) & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 18(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(31.5+t)^{0.5} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(31.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=126,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =126 \\\\ 12(31.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+0)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ 378+\\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =-252 \\\\ C & =-252(31.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)-\\frac{252(31.5)^{0.5}}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =12(31.5+t)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+t})}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $490\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=490$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =490 \\\\ 315+15{t}_{o}-5{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 315+10{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =175 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =17.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(17.5) & =12(31.5+17.5)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+17.5})}^{0.5} \\\\  & =588-252{(\\frac{31.5}{49})}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈386.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539509} {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539592} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539782} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107142" :question/problem "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539553} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539639} {:answer/id #uuid "85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539700} {:answer/id #uuid "a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539752} {:answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-269525" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58e41124-d27e-5bf8-a849-55991f19b628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539631} {:answer/id #uuid "b1098335-0f3d-532a-9ba1-59d86a55f4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539778} {:answer/id #uuid "bd65e325-f1b8-5c87-9662-6c18254e66f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539807} {:answer/id #uuid "f74d244e-220f-5aa8-ba66-446e22db924d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539897} {:answer/id #uuid "fadee215-c360-5052-9061-2ac46dc7f6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e41124-d27e-5bf8-a849-55991f19b628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539631} :answer-field/id #uuid "db152ebb-c302-5112-8c45-9948cd14ca8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "789a361b-1deb-51ca-88a0-26707116d922" :question/math-academy-id "q-65381" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e980e9415d52338c90eb5c167a32b02815237b1554f9aea06f831e88db6f3777.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $cot$ is the reciprocal of $tan.$ So first we need to compute tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{PR}{QR}=\\frac{3}{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66b5f8-e76f-5f77-8f96-098a448df87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539542} {:answer/id #uuid "22e0ee74-658c-52fe-a2b1-2df54d380881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539547} {:answer/id #uuid "38ae8a7e-8879-58a2-b77d-2f1efa7ea909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539585} {:answer/id #uuid "9579f35d-0a62-56c0-87be-1dce6c45f688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539727} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf065d6-21d7-507f-b607-cc7b49b70a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9579f35d-0a62-56c0-87be-1dce6c45f688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539727} :answer-field/id #uuid "42169019-4c90-5f92-9ed4-0e9272ff36d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9728a2a0-30c7-53d5-836b-39033ca5b896" :question/math-academy-id "q-122902" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9003434f721485b5ee3eb2ed417d0c4c68514b98060f6149bc974270e55411a.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡P=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{QR}{15}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{QR}{15}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡P={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539536} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc6a18-26ef-5ee7-a216-046a93109cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539634} {:answer/id #uuid "684d8919-d630-53ec-be88-da83985b9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539650} {:answer/id #uuid "adf72623-7732-5460-b0b6-5cc1011adb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539775} {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894} :answer-field/id #uuid "746714c2-cc0f-5a43-9606-b934d8793273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" :question/math-academy-id "q-67435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c322d1d5-fa93-5b4d-95c0-830b54df1e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c322d1d5-fa93-5b4d-95c0-830b54df1e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540494} :answer-field/id #uuid "672d067c-a7f8-5d32-b7dd-2a9c164ead57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837c5d7e-5c7a-55c0-a1dd-2c6a1f5a7c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837c5d7e-5c7a-55c0-a1dd-2c6a1f5a7c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540472} :answer-field/id #uuid "71bcb1f3-c0ec-5af1-9ecb-8c82c317c35c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674927f3-92ff-5d26-a00d-cb0a1f3ad243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674927f3-92ff-5d26-a00d-cb0a1f3ad243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540467} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdcf7e5-dd52-509d-8532-451e7e4957a8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c34ce35-aef6-5c54-826e-13e18dd80841" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨3,\\frac{7}{2},-2⟩$ and $v=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-1,5⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & \\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((\\frac{7}{2})(5)-(-2)(-1))i-((3)(5)-(-2)(\\frac{1}{2}))j+((3)(-1)-(\\frac{7}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{35}{2}-(2))i-(15-(-1))j+(-3-(\\frac{7}{4}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}i-16j-\\frac{19}{4}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{31}{2},-16,-\\frac{19}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083370b1-ba72-5090-86d4-3a98f815ca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-\\frac{79}{10},\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540442} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa1026d-b21a-538c-9efb-fc6dfba4a3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540455} {:answer/id #uuid "83d427ec-a05b-57c1-b3a1-26cb9989f5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,-\\frac{121}{10},\\frac{23}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad06b859-0b73-5512-aaa2-eb46767b7e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540486} {:answer/id #uuid "c03d7484-43b2-53d9-a04d-756366cee69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad06b859-0b73-5512-aaa2-eb46767b7e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540486} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a5d5e9-93a6-5b25-801f-3e73c6bcbbe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8258bec8-eb13-513a-bb18-127d6cd09d35" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨\\frac{3}{5},2,5⟩$ and $v=⟨2,1,\\frac{7}{2}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 5 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(\\frac{7}{2})-(5)(1))i-((\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{7}{2})-(5)(2))j+((\\frac{3}{5})(1)-(2)(2))k \\\\  & =(7-(5))i-(\\frac{21}{10}-(10))j+(\\frac{3}{5}-(4))k \\\\  & =2i+\\frac{79}{10}j-\\frac{17}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7523b92-e0fa-5100-89df-7dae96eaf50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7523b92-e0fa-5100-89df-7dae96eaf50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540485} :answer-field/id #uuid "03642954-db1f-5b56-baba-c60918de52e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154386c8-e60b-57cd-89b8-ad30585cdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154386c8-e60b-57cd-89b8-ad30585cdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540444} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20ad369-865c-5616-8eee-c0227b79e977" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b45ff0-de68-500f-ae65-ebd7d951b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b45ff0-de68-500f-ae65-ebd7d951b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540459} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda0061c-c650-5039-a061-eeffb1ce737c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e7674f-c68b-5ba3-8599-974d4b1beac2" :question/problem "Let $u=4i+2j-\\frac{4}{3}k$ and $v=-4i+4j+\\frac{1}{3}k.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(\\frac{1}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{3})(4))i-((4)(\\frac{1}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{3})(-4))j+((4)(4)-(2)(-4))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}))i-(\\frac{4}{3}-(\\frac{16}{3}))j+(16-(-8))k \\\\  & =6i+4j+24k \\\\  & =⟨6,4,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af1c12e-b138-529d-9912-81429586885e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{25}{4},\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540461} {:answer/id #uuid "66be531f-acfc-566c-952f-5c82b25ac6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540465} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38e1f-ca4e-5edd-80e9-760d59c37bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540484} {:answer/id #uuid "c555375d-64f4-5eb0-baea-1a6328f3c1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540495} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb70fa6-35b2-5b98-9141-cdcd799d17f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{13}{4},-\\frac{15}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38e1f-ca4e-5edd-80e9-760d59c37bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540484} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d0eee8-8d34-5f0a-b375-50554cb9ecc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf9484b3-9081-5c6e-811b-eb353ca9f662" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-1,-1,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨-\\frac{5}{2},1,\\frac{5}{4}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)(\\frac{5}{4})-(-2)(1))i-((-1)(\\frac{5}{4})-(-2)(-\\frac{5}{2}))j+((-1)(1)-(-1)(-\\frac{5}{2}))k \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}-(-2))i-(-\\frac{5}{4}-(5))j+(-1-(\\frac{5}{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}i+\\frac{25}{4}j-\\frac{7}{2}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240d3498-fa54-5157-88f1-3818e0422e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i-\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540450} {:answer/id #uuid "27ea1601-7690-5806-b2f8-3fec36a8db4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8i+\\frac{107}{10}j-\\frac{11}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540453} {:answer/id #uuid "85523c49-77fc-5c77-be0e-f3d65c058fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{10}i+\\frac{59}{5}j+16k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540475} {:answer/id #uuid "c8659aa4-9b80-5734-ad39-99d9e9608d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16i-\\frac{37}{10}j-\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540498} {:answer/id #uuid "da155be7-0d97-5290-b3c8-388435680f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da155be7-0d97-5290-b3c8-388435680f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540502} :answer-field/id #uuid "c298c57a-7dab-5682-bf12-994f4328b57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c879baa4-8bcb-559c-9780-708721266a3e" :question/problem "Let $u=-\\frac{6}{5}i+2j+k$ and $v=-\\frac{7}{2}i-4j+6k.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} & 2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5} & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & 6\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5} & 2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(6)-(1)(-4))i-((-\\frac{6}{5})(6)-(1)(-\\frac{7}{2}))j+((-\\frac{6}{5})(-4)-(2)(-\\frac{7}{2}))k \\\\  & =(12-(-4))i-(-\\frac{36}{5}-(-\\frac{7}{2}))j+(\\frac{24}{5}-(-7))k \\\\  & =16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨16,\\frac{37}{10},\\frac{59}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725} :answer-field/id #uuid "796d2edf-72cd-5edb-a250-8f0040849fed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e75bed-6305-557a-ac6f-4a738a0fec12" :question/math-academy-id "q-133287" :question/problem "The eagle's path traces a curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane. Suppose that the eagle is at the point $P$ on $C$ at time $t.$ Expressed in terms of $t,$ what function gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P?$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $C$ at time $t$ equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to $C$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a634dcb-2ecf-54f4-96f1-be7f5d63e9a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dfc8af8-b45c-5404-a021-aa0515c46816" :question/math-academy-id "q-352254" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-4+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=4.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x(-4+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-4x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi ((-2({4}^{2})+\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}({4}^{4}))-(-2+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{45}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f4b480b-46bc-5ad0-b2dc-179d612978aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s^2-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f4b480b-46bc-5ad0-b2dc-179d612978aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s^2-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1abf10fb-79e5-50b9-8eca-b49f881c098e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24075bbd-fad1-5e70-847c-3b96960e39c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333072" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0=-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & =-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2 & ={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2(\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2s(s+1)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2({s}^{2}+s)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c2faca-0edb-5ef8-8326-5900825078b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{3/5}}{2}{x}^{11/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540697} {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} {:answer/id #uuid "da7a61db-d353-5a53-b318-1be353f60fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{31}^{2/5}}{32}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540895} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e9b62-8e00-5346-a13f-699ec44a7450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{7}^{2/5}}{2}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540927} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfa7707-765d-5937-8a04-fb11b50efc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{36}^{2/5}}{72}{x}^{12/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} :answer-field/id #uuid "05367c1f-7c48-5001-bd7f-8cd94f9a1ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31f8bbd7-44e6-58a8-9f8d-def7c80fe8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29652" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{(\\frac{12}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} {:answer/id #uuid "56903a87-08bb-5210-b21a-15bdc9bdba3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540671} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb90bb5-f5d6-5864-b41d-7da56b21a5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540720} {:answer/id #uuid "bba99957-7e8d-5791-b9fd-9c58160a4cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540843} {:answer/id #uuid "c0463d14-2b9e-5fba-b2c4-9802c27cf249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} :answer-field/id #uuid "464a658d-9140-58a9-ae22-53b8f7f33f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a65a87-c8d0-5bbf-adbe-0789f4e08164" :question/math-academy-id "q-336500" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank at a bottling plant can hold up to $220\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of syrup solution with $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated sugar solution with concentration $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8.4-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=8.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 8.4 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =8.4(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =8.4(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 8.4(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =8.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ 2.8(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ 84+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-60 \\\\ C & =-60(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)-\\frac{60(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2.8(30+t)-60{(\\frac{30}{30+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $220\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=220$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =220 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2.8(30+25)-60{(\\frac{30}{30+25})}^{2} \\\\  & =154-60{(\\frac{30}{55})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈136.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222b62ab-1bfb-546b-aab5-03561e3c701f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540586} {:answer/id #uuid "262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540595} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc77f0f-f146-5ca7-b666-ad8985b69724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540635} {:answer/id #uuid "e0610972-c22b-5f94-966b-2d4ed2ec3bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540910} {:answer/id #uuid "e423743e-6949-5391-8f90-a809db7277ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540595} :answer-field/id #uuid "030979c4-2ad6-5121-addd-f709ea3b08d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40216ba0-427a-539f-9353-a0236ab249d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85235" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd812d1d33801a3ce8368feca0e7d6b87e02bcfab5d904d9b59172d95d0204e5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074012c5-6ca1-5495-8009-0d0c255d3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540534} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc800b9-5ce9-51df-9646-ad28b4b5f5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540575} {:answer/id #uuid "81f7e7be-4c3c-5db4-9e89-a897730e6bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{155}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540748} {:answer/id #uuid "afc9329d-23eb-511f-aef5-7c8e3284646b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540823} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a0d10-9bd4-5e8e-9023-0b2590d9b92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "074012c5-6ca1-5495-8009-0d0c255d3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a192929-90b9-5ade-b716-7719dbfc5bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59606949-3c87-521e-a2c2-60c040472354" :question/math-academy-id "q-109956" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=16$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=6.$ Find $\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y]={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}\\cdot 16+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 6=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540602} {:answer/id #uuid "434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540643} {:answer/id #uuid "602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540692} {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t/2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} :answer-field/id #uuid "09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138966" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,4].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053c1f5-d753-5d4f-9ed6-23685e190240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1053c1f5-d753-5d4f-9ed6-23685e190240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1265d9c-4abc-5c77-ab0f-534e48c095b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65741bd8-a817-5dbe-b425-cf51f2c41352" :question/math-academy-id "q-223688" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+5)(2x+k)$ is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{9}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+5)(2x+k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5+k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5+k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5+k}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 5+k & =9 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10217f51-caf3-5dd4-96ac-8656fdc36c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540556} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbc2cd9-05ee-5ab2-b7f8-ecea5083e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540626} {:answer/id #uuid "4655fb84-1019-5281-8d52-c7b7aa2b05c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540649} {:answer/id #uuid "80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540744} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0e0e46-2dfb-5983-8032-25be5dcb57b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540744} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b703d74-20ff-5a85-bdd0-120e273b7e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37897" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} {:answer/id #uuid "52c67f33-11db-51b1-9650-af1cf2900407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20928a6-6ffa-5ddc-9782-9f840a1c74b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540798} {:answer/id #uuid "aca174a9-712f-5452-958f-e15b38456d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540818} {:answer/id #uuid "d7682d19-2baf-59af-84e2-dbac5b3c49b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e476d3-efaf-5ec7-9cea-2c3e7d2d49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c55e9d5-5244-5391-84ab-9850b2ba8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-336508" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment tank at a chemical plant can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater with $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. A more concentrated wastewater stream with concentration $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =15\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =5(30+15)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =225-110{(\\frac{30}{45})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈176.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16e017f-d9d9-54ce-97a3-a549b27da1ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e20ee7e-741b-5069-a51c-7de25cc32da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133285" :question/problem "Calculate the horizontal velocity of the eagle as a function of time.\n\nThe horizontal velocity of the bird is$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by ${x}^{′}(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5}).\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(t/5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}/25}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by the function ${x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5800f9f-a331-5cd2-b8d9-dac2799c0d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "742d438b-e3dc-52b0-a068-888e70c6be15" :question/math-academy-id "q-133290" :question/problem "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$y(\\frac{110}{23})≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y(\\frac{110}{23})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{110}{23}) & ≈L(\\frac{110}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(-\\frac{5}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1})-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69712d4c-92b7-5f9d-abcd-ca7ad46c6567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^2+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69712d4c-92b7-5f9d-abcd-ca7ad46c6567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^2+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8de0228-5ef5-53a6-b77b-949275d4025a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ca91093-1f1e-55da-a457-e3e1666bf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-333069" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}$ ." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{2}(t)+1\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-3 & =-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =3+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4{X}_{2}(s)-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4(\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)})-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4(3s+1)-s(s+2)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{12s+4-({s}^{2}+2s)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b5785c-9c86-58fa-8702-7b20eaf94717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540600} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d02e90-1b24-51db-a87e-21fe5d5fa85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540804} {:answer/id #uuid "da8619fc-53ce-586e-8d07-73efa17d3b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540896} {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} {:answer/id #uuid "f746d0ee-4d45-5138-a444-dd8fb7c426b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa476148-4520-535e-8de8-c555998b437f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0b25f6-0e44-56a6-a167-f47173302a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-113672" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{4/5}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{(\\frac{9}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac7ed820-a9a3-5250-8205-8b2479f31381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540815} :answer-field/id #uuid "16743b63-9c0b-507f-81e5-a93679be7ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0beb016-ee67-53e9-a1ae-c396296738c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352258" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-6+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=2$ to $x=3.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}x(-6+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}(-6x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(-3(3)^{2}+\\frac{5(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4})-(-3(2)^{2}+\\frac{5(2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f184934c-5ddc-5c09-89b2-c3b9969b08ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540930} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cb8eed7-dd98-5bd2-8807-1eee6e64325d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b40e7c83-4aaa-5842-b161-090408598820" :question/math-academy-id "q-279714" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(3\\le X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4}){(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5-4} \\\\  & =10{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1} \\\\  & ≈0.658\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8241bb7f-109e-55f9-be72-8316a22c2b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8241bb7f-109e-55f9-be72-8316a22c2b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540751} :answer-field/id #uuid "9da18180-5a36-5125-8324-5c1b0c6c8774" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b506000a-3cca-5384-98ce-1df2cd5d8561" :question/math-academy-id "q-223671" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(x+7)(2x-5)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the equation $y=-(x+7)(2x-5),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-7+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{14}{2}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{9}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36064afd-4624-50aa-b9f7-869ddad593ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540613} {:answer/id #uuid "3e30535b-824e-5d84-96a9-2fa046e67302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540632} {:answer/id #uuid "576b7268-d013-5459-8484-febaf9a3d59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd73e85-3ada-52db-9939-f4f819c081ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} :answer-field/id #uuid "889a0978-0193-5e72-b84c-66f0c08de47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6915e10-1543-50b0-a358-2c56a1366444" :question/math-academy-id "q-336744" :question/problem "A fermentation tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of minerals enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *minerals* enter the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}(18-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 18\\,(\\frac{18-t}{18})-2.2{(\\frac{18-t}{18})}^{3} \\\\  & =7.2\\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})-2.2{(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =7.2,\\beta =-2.2,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =7.2-(-2.2)=9.4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de636aa5-cf99-5e6d-9779-b79decf69364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.337" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540907} :answer-field/id #uuid "66359473-563b-5acf-a793-afdba80eb7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf9b3381-1894-58e7-8430-4bb3204c16fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-279683" :question/problem "If $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X\\le 1)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(5,\\frac{2}{5}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X\\in \\{0,1\\}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5-1} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{5}+5{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =0.337\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4662ff5c-1779-587c-8818-862f89cec631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540650} {:answer/id #uuid "57b912a8-d2ae-5a4c-a949-6c6b3b4e2b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540676} {:answer/id #uuid "78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540731} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3c24fb-cfb1-5483-9975-5fe28ec66e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540791} {:answer/id #uuid "c78c0d6a-5d3e-5010-92bc-68ad33e022bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78b7c07c-ec2e-5b2b-8de0-b1f928e490af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540731} :answer-field/id #uuid "e825c5c1-d92a-52fc-8cd7-189d7de3d71e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d952daa8-27a5-56d3-8d17-8e3ce2fc3807" :question/math-academy-id "q-124785" :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca003331d04113119019996f15296ec2ab16fa0c14227a12f5f421c4c4103746.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f0c1c9df42fc2ed772353baec0f81e796485d8bf3c1cd9dde234e74e39a04c56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7\\cdot 2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089a813c-6c0e-5e0b-8e37-0be57131cd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540541} {:answer/id #uuid "1a442bb6-9973-5336-9114-49d50247246c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}y\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540570} {:answer/id #uuid "3b737ea3-db83-533e-bf9c-6d4d23c19b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(2y+5)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540624} {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b74f6-b844-5d38-9b76-3abb89103049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+5y+1}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42dcd5e7-1244-58d1-8b4c-96176ca2b293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540641} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfcd94c6-4a82-5d36-a7d1-d10e9ebad35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd63787d-4bed-5aec-9b0b-525bc4b676f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-109259" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9ad43f4bddaeab8327e5405193471c005962013355ec84c4a6f858640216dd7.png)\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}-5y-4$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}-5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=-4$ to $y=-1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)=-2y-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(-{y}^{2}-5y-4)\\sqrt{1+{(-2y-5)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{26+20y+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}({y}^{2}+5y+4)\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+10y+13}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541150 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08723c80-e899-5f73-9042-d123c4781581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540539} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0422d8-ab75-5b1f-88c6-2c6d1cf32970" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3134f95-ccde-508a-8ade-bbcbdfc93226" :question/math-academy-id "q-344647" :question/problem "Find the average value ${g}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $g(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\pi /4-(-\\pi /4))}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }[(-\\sqrt{2})-(\\sqrt{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c60dd5-4f2f-5247-b508-e4412be02a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540888} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f220aba-10fa-5140-91ce-bb3c746dbdc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4a5e43e-e7a6-55b0-a709-f89ec3a86933" :question/math-academy-id "q-133293" :question/problem "Let $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ be the position vector of the eagle. Find the distance between the eagle and its nest at time $t=5\\,\\text{s},$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe eagle is approximately$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$ away from its nest after $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "Since the nest is at the origin, the distance between the bird and its nest at time $t=5$ is $|r(5)|.$\n\nWe know that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3}.$ Evaluating the function at $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{5}),\\frac{95}{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{95}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(5)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+{(\\frac{95}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9\\,025}{9}} \\\\  & ≈31.68\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e8b185-9e4d-524a-98a5-45013ad83009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540543} {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540544} {:answer/id #uuid "5cae022b-6032-5575-aa99-3439709b9c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540684} {:answer/id #uuid "624013de-ac75-5b7a-a4d8-e9822947abad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540694} {:answer/id #uuid "6f15d79f-35df-5dfd-909e-20ba976f3a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6e02d6-f190-5b77-94b7-25463dc18fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540544} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d7d6654-0ebf-57f1-bbf5-229f64325368" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb5c025a-0d14-591c-9102-8c187483f1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-85233" :question/problem "![](images/75/751d38a1a37afd277cbd54850abf61db898a231b49ad0d4bcaa5964bbab601e1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{4}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/05/0534847b2363ff75e053b9d05736ff367f2ad6da527b7100de0018d003c4c9df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{5})}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c25b5e8-29a2-5d6a-9195-b39381be0aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 6 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541245} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b961-8e87-550d-a8e0-0ee0a3e468f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 t + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541249} {:answer/id #uuid "9a77f260-9187-5326-8c4a-24a29bc84c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541253} {:answer/id #uuid "a0404454-ff13-50be-8ab6-15207e83bcf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541254} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a7ebb6-60db-5509-bee6-228908d07bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 - \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c25b5e8-29a2-5d6a-9195-b39381be0aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 6 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541245} :answer-field/id #uuid "907eeef4-8e8b-5f0c-8e61-37d6501dc067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14f8b19c-03b3-520d-8d8f-1445fd24196c" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}4 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 t + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 + \\frac{2}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b6f872-a2b6-51b5-91ce-9e9804ef80bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 4 t e^{t} - 4 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541235} {:answer/id #uuid "46bc6725-4437-5018-af20-90eb8255f285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 t e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541240} {:answer/id #uuid "6102edf7-bb37-51f8-ac13-c1becf20b623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 4 t e^{t} - 4 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{2}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541246} {:answer/id #uuid "74a3f346-66c3-5125-bb1c-f79e2e4599f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 4 t e^{t} + 4 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541247} {:answer/id #uuid "9203d069-6357-50d2-a652-33020f0f9f0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{4}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46bc6725-4437-5018-af20-90eb8255f285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - 2 t^{2} e^{t} - 8 t e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541240} :answer-field/id #uuid "179914be-111c-5b2b-83ab-1e8d35c73d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "530874a1-6640-5e7f-9a0e-6e65871c39a9" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + 2 t - 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)=2 t + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + 2 t - 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 \\left(2 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 2}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ - 2 \\left(t^{2} + 2 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + 2 t - 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 6 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ 2 t \\left(- t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2303f608-48b7-5784-990e-095149d5db5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1 + \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} + 6 e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541238} {:answer/id #uuid "540436d5-d613-53ad-a74e-19e0ecec9b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 - \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541243} {:answer/id #uuid "a4edab06-cf8e-5a76-b2d8-e59ca8e4c993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 12 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541255} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c01684-3333-58d5-b391-8be426accc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 6 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541263} {:answer/id #uuid "f696a871-8842-5610-9f5c-2f864cd239ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- t - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 9 e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4edab06-cf8e-5a76-b2d8-e59ca8e4c993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 3 t^{2} e^{t} - 9 t e^{t} - 12 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541255} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c801dc-024c-575a-b842-e243d4bc3807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fe06042-8aa0-5170-bdc7-f300c6962914" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 3)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{1}{t} \\\\ - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t} \\\\ - 3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - t - \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 1 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ 3 \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 4\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac5cf81-07cf-5d63-a1dd-e7ba4f943e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ -1 - \\frac{1}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541237} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3188b0-d884-5377-ab31-f4dae48d4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 2 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541241} {:answer/id #uuid "c20cd946-fa72-5557-a918-b39037346b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 + \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} \\\\ 3 + \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541264} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e8a05b-0454-5d97-ad03-f310df1208a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - 2 t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541267} {:answer/id #uuid "fac3b51a-060c-5220-8c81-494b3f2428c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - 2 t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 2 + \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c3188b0-d884-5377-ab31-f4dae48d4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} - 2 e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541241} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfd4fa90-6aec-5417-a617-5719d0fd4b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af315a0d-cdb8-50ea-94f3-8aca5e2aec09" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} + t + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} + t + 1)=2 t + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} + t + 1)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ - e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ - \\left(2 t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t + 1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right)}{t} \\\\ - \\left(t^{2} + t + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} + t + 1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t - 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 - \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ \\left(- t^{2} - 3 t - 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e01a93-bfdd-5d60-b008-8c0b11015eed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 t + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - 3 t e^{t} + 2 e^{t} \\\\ -1 + \\frac{3}{t} - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541234} {:answer/id #uuid "3d02bfeb-b1f1-5e6c-b4a5-b5137a3b7eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541239} {:answer/id #uuid "56d49eae-7ec5-5872-b426-29429faf867e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 + \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ - t^{2} e^{t} + 5 t e^{t} - 5 e^{t} \\\\ 3 - \\frac{6}{t} + \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541244} {:answer/id #uuid "a594b3fd-3990-519f-9b9e-837f8cfe919d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ 2 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ 2 - \\frac{3}{t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541256} {:answer/id #uuid "cb52a2d0-993c-52aa-a62e-df68c4e7cfdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 + \\frac{9}{t} \\\\ 2 t e^{t} - 3 e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t} + \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d02bfeb-b1f1-5e6c-b4a5-b5137a3b7eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ t^{2} e^{t} - t e^{t} - e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541239} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a83f6e-4bfe-53c6-99e0-f32bda4ce4df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6931a3c-17b8-55a5-9c5d-652d29b81597" :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=t^{2} - 3 t + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $u^{′}(t)$ and $f^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{′}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\u^{′}(t)&=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)=2 t - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′}&=u^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t)f^{′}(t)\\\\&=(2 t - 3)\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+(t^{2} - 3 t + 2)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3}{t} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 3 \\left(2 t - 3\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\left(2 t - 3\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{2 t - 3}{t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{3 \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right)}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - 3 t + 2\\right) e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{t^{2} - 3 t + 2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- 6 t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 t + 9 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 9 - \\frac{6}{t} \\\\ \\left(t^{2} - t - 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ 1 - \\frac{2}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0caec6d9-3660-5e5d-93f8-273e3177e9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541450} {:answer/id #uuid "37dbe3a0-18a2-5453-a5d4-551f3906a5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541542} {:answer/id #uuid "99a09e67-57a3-595e-8038-9fd0c9ae9d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541742} {:answer/id #uuid "c4bf4cb6-3664-5e42-9eac-88072f5bc8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541836} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5bae98-5138-5fd6-bec2-d20b4cb20409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0caec6d9-3660-5e5d-93f8-273e3177e9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ec3545-9073-58ce-bcc7-3fa4b6d955fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c69e568-3aa6-590d-a547-523c7522fc27" :question/math-academy-id "q-141252" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 1)}{(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/1018201037350f6bd231a384219d03c03fc1e2165a088e158c9ba2b2bfbb1022.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27db9b54-964b-54ac-bbac-bbad8d91280d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541505} {:answer/id #uuid "321aa89f-4cc2-54c3-89b7-8028876aeb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541526} {:answer/id #uuid "78247b87-c63b-59e2-9881-7f7af5d616aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541671} {:answer/id #uuid "86ec3eb1-3f81-55ae-aee0-50eda1b75a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541695} {:answer/id #uuid "beaf8493-9a78-55da-98d2-762d26ef1c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321aa89f-4cc2-54c3-89b7-8028876aeb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541526} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e86af00-a67b-5794-9225-1dd62d11259f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1873fa45-940e-5434-9a04-82891d309ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100180" :question/problem "Calculate the value of ${(-\\frac{2}{9})}^{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{9})}^{0}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "496974f1-cd7b-5abe-b03b-8caf3c2ef7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541574} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df6c47b-c813-591d-800c-7da989a956a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "316d8b52-05f5-5660-9987-6f2357a674ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-342789" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542142 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa4e6a0-3aeb-559f-96d7-bd92f700b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541445} {:answer/id #uuid "b490045f-fa26-53d0-83e1-8ad88e404827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541793} {:answer/id #uuid "b7410f52-b281-5541-8a42-cabcaa3e3486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541800} {:answer/id #uuid "fdae0bda-2f60-5c1b-bf1e-e3f18a773891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541954} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f995b-cc21-5f36-9851-e5fb6941662d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aa4e6a0-3aeb-559f-96d7-bd92f700b296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541445} :answer-field/id #uuid "41165c67-5f21-5f55-b678-99fd5befca9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e631ed-e489-57e1-8328-8ad962002fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-75382" :question/problem "$3+\\frac{3}{2}x-1-\\frac{7}{4}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+\\frac{3x}{2}-1-\\frac{7x}{4} &= (\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{7x}{4})+(3-1) \\\\ &= (\\frac{6}{4}-\\frac{7}{4})x+(3-1) \\\\ &= (\\frac{6-7}{4})x+2 \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ad0bb0-44b4-5377-b18a-d503eaa1b661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541440} {:answer/id #uuid "2399beaf-eace-5a34-9002-6ad316000d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541496} {:answer/id #uuid "bd150fa0-ce1f-5591-a343-840beb560c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541817} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ccf14d-454f-5062-8149-cc1e7174890e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541942} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8ca8d0-5bd2-5279-a5a0-2e48e66c5361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd150fa0-ce1f-5591-a343-840beb560c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541817} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea5f0102-7a6b-5740-bb7e-b8c83d6d91f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b160342-1c9b-5cb6-9f7b-8bf55c5af2c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141356" :question/problem "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0}{(2\\cdot 0-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8f39669eaedc42a26d28d96f6dbb961080816e7fadd182b79a260c8de27b3d1a.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542144 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf16f7e4-17c9-549f-a864-943b15c55740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf16f7e4-17c9-549f-a864-943b15c55740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541860} :answer-field/id #uuid "9960e886-6591-52e7-b6de-f3f58994bcca" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4234aa-c19a-50c4-8afe-1f256cd2c1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4234aa-c19a-50c4-8afe-1f256cd2c1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541749} :answer-field/id #uuid "c603d277-c436-515e-9401-4e14d4e32737" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bd1b924-fea7-5666-9b66-85d3682d3092" :question/math-academy-id "q-362903" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b2/b2485a1b1386468904947c52ff3f52997f72597da84384ae41d246f386a39232.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7af24af-4c8d-5f43-b558-8db7ccd96709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7af24af-4c8d-5f43-b558-8db7ccd96709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541939} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f4ee068-a4ba-5955-9803-7974762ed968" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "651d48b6-dcdd-5bc2-87b4-a66ffecf04ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-175589" :question/problem "If $\\frac{1}{15}=-\\frac{m}{3},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nThen, we remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{3} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{m}{3}) & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is being divided by $3.$ To isolate $m$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{m}{3} & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ \\frac{3m}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}m}{\\cancel{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15787c24-e618-53d4-8a29-3dc9499996c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.804" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541465} {:answer/id #uuid "581302f3-81bc-5a00-aa69-41b710ebfdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541597} {:answer/id #uuid "59eed6a5-45fc-5d51-a620-a7b381132dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541600} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f0c17b-c929-51a0-8a87-56cae3e64293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541908} {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e663f0c8-fcdd-52e4-bb43-ebf22bfa305c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.824" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541911} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0898c05-a5e3-5618-a8b5-3c67837f18a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b7ee989-a51c-50ad-8da0-87452c898ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-8837" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+9 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-9 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{9}{2})=-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…≈-1.352\\,127\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $-1.352\\,127$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+\\pi =1.789\\,465\\,272\\,…≈1.789\\,465 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+2\\pi =4.931\\,057\\,926\\,…≈4.931\\,058\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.789\\,466\\cdot 4.931\\,058≈8.824\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4f18e3-c2c7-5633-ad51-327c11dda76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541451} {:answer/id #uuid "1742151a-fdad-55b5-83cf-1eac826f3b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541470} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb12aa8-c236-5848-bf31-4b538dca5489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541648} {:answer/id #uuid "b235bce1-5f22-5ec4-b929-1a8d2bf6ab80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541788} {:answer/id #uuid "f97c1141-ae12-5ca0-8969-0f2ba9d5bac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f97c1141-ae12-5ca0-8969-0f2ba9d5bac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541945} :answer-field/id #uuid "2224a384-1d96-5035-96d8-8e3991487107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a9df102-8e4d-542b-ad62-072cc2469e7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-146287" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|3}=\\frac{22}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{X|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}+{6}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{9}]-{(\\frac{22}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+45-\\frac{484}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc71f80-e83e-56bd-a43b-28c93a53e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541779} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5c497b-6bdc-534b-b9f6-c27062181e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541826} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b36070-55de-58e5-8e82-45b02bf82b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541834} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e096ad-fe8e-5767-ba62-fa5fd8b382c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541840} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa1dbde-f325-5791-9cf0-8c99ff494b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf5c497b-6bdc-534b-b9f6-c27062181e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541826} :answer-field/id #uuid "099ae849-575f-5a4b-9f92-63b55d7f04a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "be4431be-3fe9-5dd2-82ee-71868596c05f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103815" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fdcad9-364a-5070-981d-f9c1b4551dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541430} {:answer/id #uuid "61f54b36-6f68-535c-92ab-0bc07509609f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541623} {:answer/id #uuid "89c62352-327c-574f-9565-b27be34bf0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\sqrt{3}x-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541699} {:answer/id #uuid "c725779e-c768-516a-9762-dfbab4f6c814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541843} {:answer/id #uuid "e8050d30-bcad-51d5-8a1a-40058eb95f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8050d30-bcad-51d5-8a1a-40058eb95f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541916} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2a0e8c-7f88-5fbd-86b3-0d022972fd45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7a90aa7-52b9-549e-8c2d-341971525e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-141419" :question/problem "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0b04158944bd15aeea3220d4aa63321dd39c423a4f6646ba59c299fa8ef736.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6726d53-1c49-55d4-a9fc-abfb02d98019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6726d53-1c49-55d4-a9fc-abfb02d98019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541766} :answer-field/id #uuid "da69ddf7-0c95-5610-bb80-afdff6c65e1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bae7493b-97fa-5e69-b993-6f9d41cd3185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bae7493b-97fa-5e69-b993-6f9d41cd3185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541809} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb8b6806-7bed-5b6d-a52c-5d124c4f7580" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c83da1a3-053f-5553-bc32-57bdb774a02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-351547" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}} $x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4ee331-238e-59a4-8986-69bac9c080bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541449} {:answer/id #uuid "2198478a-0906-5c0b-a84e-218df9b6d508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541493} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9b6e35-f362-5186-9453-fac9e3e6cf34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541512} {:answer/id #uuid "579c0c58-6b75-506e-975b-78e02a08fb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,184" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541596} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9e46f32-f5c9-506e-89ac-1af46cb2bd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\,148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541849} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7b075f-829a-5526-980c-c8864059ac56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9ae048b-af0f-521f-b439-497b724959ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-21475" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{15}{4})=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}≈{75.069}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince ${75.069}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={75.069}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘}=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}≈255.069\\,{…}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $75.069\\cdot 255.069≈19\\,148$\nrounded to the nearest integer."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a44896-e16c-55e0-b031-ed3c4525e491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15a44896-e16c-55e0-b031-ed3c4525e491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541466} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b01fc71-15e0-5989-a945-a229e3e4981a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441f08b4-04dd-5e42-838c-9e7b01251fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "441f08b4-04dd-5e42-838c-9e7b01251fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541567} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea70e8a0-cc70-507c-a9ef-fbc409c64de9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1dd610f-bcb9-5742-82dd-3d71c2887869" :question/math-academy-id "q-362883" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e7020a-b4c1-5c1e-970a-f3080b2f18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542312} {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb7ea70-ab48-5b5f-a35f-b10fa2c57fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542339} {:answer/id #uuid "e07e6d25-a555-5fcd-a7a4-bc176e9d0fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542341} {:answer/id #uuid "e433ebda-2236-542b-93b3-4804ba472bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a21fd961-41aa-514a-84a6-bd53640d329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542319} :answer-field/id #uuid "08cce96c-e9ed-58e4-b741-d8a8f993b454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02bf06bd-a114-5709-91cd-398487459c7f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,4)$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+4\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+4\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+4\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(4-2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=4-2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-2t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(4-2t) \\\\  & =-5t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-5t+2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035fa252-c497-5441-a7a9-995b1b999642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542283} {:answer/id #uuid "1931c9f8-d41a-5ef1-b13b-ee9bd8a5e925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542289} {:answer/id #uuid "940e3279-20b0-5c97-a20b-20bc1ccb29af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542313} {:answer/id #uuid "c5abffee-6393-58b4-acdb-d0f5a1c85331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542331} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0947a-9d73-5217-8106-27e3a8efa0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542338} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbe6447-d7b2-5aa3-832a-868998a34289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fca5dad-31ea-55b7-94df-b638c15ad9f2" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-2t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-2t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "473e8524-7399-51a9-981f-c0aa4668dd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542297} {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d23fa3-0d57-5274-baa4-eef4412f5a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{577}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542333} {:answer/id #uuid "d58a6523-8175-5758-9a7a-5450d554c95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542337} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ab0414-6ef1-5f05-b520-6b9c768d227c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-24t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c04afd16-987b-5ec2-af26-9f9b902a2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542328} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d4fa989-2ac8-5a30-824a-30041569e8ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b732e53-9da1-5d2f-ab3b-dfb9b5fc9cae" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(-1,- \\frac{11}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(-i-\\frac{11}{3}\\,j)-(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(3\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-4t+3)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-4t+3$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-4t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-\\frac{1}{6}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(- \\frac{1}{6})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-4t+3)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{6}t) \\\\  & =16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{577}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-\\frac{143}{6}t+\\frac{25}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20b7f03-488d-57c7-b9f2-d236742a9961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-3t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542324} {:answer/id #uuid "b77786f1-adbc-5af1-8f60-843037465c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{7}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542326} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} {:answer/id #uuid "f5361eea-4400-528a-850e-0ba9c82a1977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542347} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1096ed-014c-57c2-ab1e-36b976f89bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{277}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc8d6535-5549-51c3-b2b4-d17b02464990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542332} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e14137-0d58-55a7-bd84-61bca100a01f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "621c7226-fc49-5cd6-ab81-d4bf845859cb" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,\\frac{13}{3})$ to $(- \\frac{5}{2},2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j$ and $b=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t[(-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+2\\,j)-(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)+t(-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)\\,i+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-\\frac{3}{2}t-1$ and $y=\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t)j \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-\\frac{7}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(- \\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(- \\frac{7}{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(-\\frac{3}{2}t-1)+(\\frac{13}{3}-\\frac{7}{3}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{277}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{16}{3}t+\\frac{7}{3})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c87f5e-5e1e-533e-a685-95f4e3d70e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542288} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc5e25-69f2-53fc-bfbb-ac186ba68379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542295} {:answer/id #uuid "c42403a1-9e8a-5de5-882b-c347f626e69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542329} {:answer/id #uuid "e3968766-f69b-5abb-9c51-38782ded432b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{289}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542342} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4b91717-1749-5c7a-be22-16af96b4ab7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542344} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f19d3c7-bdba-598e-85e9-a9f2767c5565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a221b842-bd46-5723-b036-3a6f86665976" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{13}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+\\frac{13}{3}\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+\\frac{4}{3}t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\cdot \\frac{17}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{23}{6}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a45f-3cb5-5c5c-82a7-b35bad7ae911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{5}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542287} {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} {:answer/id #uuid "63e0ed3c-b3f8-5a81-b935-2f28c7723aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542307} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88aef5-06bc-5eb8-bf58-ee6e0e624524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-12t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542323} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aa4113-237e-5e1a-ac67-eb9f38bf7ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30335a9e-1e6a-5240-a835-635bf13e5660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542292} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aaa0dc-bb9c-53ef-9689-89144abe766e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2a5ec51-bad1-5473-9954-53b213963bd6" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-2,- \\frac{7}{2})$ to $(1,-1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j$ and $b=i-j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i-\\frac{7}{2}\\,j)+t(3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-2)\\,i+(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t-2$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\frac{5}{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-2)^{2}-(-\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{61}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-\\frac{29}{2}t+\\frac{15}{2})\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5582abdc-4614-5e71-964a-2d19582d849c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7b5275-0ea4-503c-a862-6c7508804d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542309} {:answer/id #uuid "70d93491-99f0-5bd9-b153-73c0784e2eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542310} {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} {:answer/id #uuid "9cad3f1b-7cf7-5209-ab65-767609942832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3c73-570d-5433-98be-243f021d0f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542317} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5407b6a-3e15-5951-adb6-f6a3f6d57b0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beef72c2-a71c-5197-903a-c3c1f057b873" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(\\frac{7}{2},4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[(\\frac{7}{2}\\,i+4\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,i+(3+t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $y=3+t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{5}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3+t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+\\frac{5}{2}t)+3+t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\frac{7}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11429afc-455f-5c8a-a4df-3cac6e918fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{325}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542286} {:answer/id #uuid "51f0679e-81ac-586f-9a56-1373b928161b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542299} {:answer/id #uuid "59abc310-6605-56a9-9327-a4ec3f9272b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542302} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8df974-825a-516c-b007-9a07e0e2b6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542303} {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f06333fd-751a-5534-ab2f-57975ac048a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542345} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a67bdf-986e-5011-ab4a-8e0c1bca5fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c47f3d69-bc6d-5af9-a7d6-c2e7d42c806b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t[(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j)-(-i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j)+t(\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)\\,i+(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{5}{2}t-1$ and $y=-\\frac{5}{3}t+2$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{5}{2}}^{2}+(- \\frac{5}{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(\\frac{5}{2}t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{5}{3}t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{13}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{5}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5}{3}t-2))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bb6d25-cb28-57c5-9bd8-8b5a3ea0d8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542296} {:answer/id #uuid "69e479cd-9bf0-5481-afeb-58ec5059536d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542308} {:answer/id #uuid "9492d2db-0d16-5473-bbf2-b5753ebbfbd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542314} {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} {:answer/id #uuid "f65df61c-e6ae-5911-bed9-45d693eec2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a14126d-fa91-5d78-8230-6c1ec9664546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542316} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fcc1cb-695e-577d-916f-00bdd3ab93a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d489cb8a-37ac-5057-8882-4a94eb183baf" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{5}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fc2385-2b81-5188-bb65-8be9a6b72cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542290} {:answer/id #uuid "31d095c6-9894-5dcd-a47c-fbff4a3ac7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542293} {:answer/id #uuid "9972107c-2631-5571-a39a-29d7ef026be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{4}{3}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542315} {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ee897a-b9ef-5dd3-8d4a-48a525540411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{145}{36}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a98fbfc4-3c83-5f2b-abab-ba3e760107fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542321} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfdb0a89-889d-56b9-a2eb-f7bf05ae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f4de30-da30-5755-a176-314836964c15" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{4}{3}).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=\\frac{3}{2}t$ and $y=\\frac{4}{3}t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{3}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{3}{2}}^{2}+{\\frac{4}{3}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{145}{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{4}{3}t} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{145}}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{\\frac{17}{6}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ebd6dd6-7259-5120-97a3-66b51aeb5bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542397} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6c862a-55e0-54f9-b7f6-7a2dc48c416c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1+3\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542401} {:answer/id #uuid "b062a522-6a46-5f79-9299-18183553eb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542403} {:answer/id #uuid "f9945e02-9035-5167-8318-4361e517c387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542428} {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc158f8d-2987-5499-a8ed-77f033124c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542429} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d2c317-5999-5ae3-83ba-55833a642212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d825389-7a3e-5461-af4a-30fb0473dbe9" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=9-2\\cdot y.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=9-2\\cdot y=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le -2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (\\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}(r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}(r)^{2}((-2\\cdot r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}-2\\cdot (r)^{3}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+9\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (r)^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)+3\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((1))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ((0))^{4}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))+(3\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(3\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))-(0)+(3)-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}3-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[3\\cdot \\theta+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\cos(\\theta)]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(6\\cdot \\pi)-(0)+(\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ &=6\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542409 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dadd38-fb68-5779-a188-b784be4a6b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542368} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbee069-7a87-51d0-9392-cbeec9ec586e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542387} {:answer/id #uuid "7d83eaae-f8b5-538e-af6a-fa57c9f45461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542391} {:answer/id #uuid "a91f9201-835b-5ae6-be79-7518add5a8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542399} {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9be48c8-ddc2-53db-874a-8450e1fe1768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542411} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30d0b97-29d6-5b4a-be3d-426bd7d15400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0e834b8-fc6b-56b5-8146-fbd537f8a411" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c7fe69ab2583c066df73bbf6719e4c58fea1ed565e345bc62c11b7ebc779508b.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4=4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\int_{0}^{4}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((4)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{1}16\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=1}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((1))^{3})-(\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{16}{3})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{16}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{16}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ddfd73-e817-5e19-bf8d-dd13eb09dba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542374} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} {:answer/id #uuid "3112b487-c350-57db-82e9-a721ed41fe97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542377} {:answer/id #uuid "ae54fd97-3a30-519e-9ebd-e1fb979dbe8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542402} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d4f8e7-781a-5b4c-827e-0040738832b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6f9f77-1552-57ae-884a-192b803d96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542376} :answer-field/id #uuid "b96cd23f-37b9-504c-a679-a848317b445b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff5722e3-b420-50b2-8b72-a65be31820d3" :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the cylinder $x^2+y^2=4$ and between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint_R f(x,y,z)\\,dV=\\iiint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\quad z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/05/0505715b5e78700e63d98fc2c1db8e7d4a3074b3e06f4202de1ce5a6831f7571.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1=1.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta,z):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta<2\\cdot \\pi,\\ 0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R 4\\cdot \\sqrt{(x)^{2}+(y)^{2}}\\,dV &=\\iiint_\\Delta (4\\cdot \\sqrt{(r\\cdot \\cos(\\theta))^2+(r\\cdot \\sin(\\theta))^2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}((1)-(0))\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\int_0^{2}4\\cdot (r)^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}[\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot (r)^{3}]_{r=0}^{r=2}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((2))^{3})-(\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot ((0))^{3})\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}(\\frac{32}{3})-(0)\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2\\cdot \\pi}\\frac{32}{3}\\,d\\theta \\\\ &=[\\frac{32}{3}\\cdot \\theta]_{\\theta=0}^{\\theta=2\\cdot \\pi} \\\\ &=(\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi)-(0) \\\\ &=\\frac{64}{3}\\cdot \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8160d239-5719-5314-9b34-25360242df8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542577} {:answer/id #uuid "9618b0be-10e0-54af-bddc-144790bcb25d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542583} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa66a-4ae8-5eb8-a19d-10e8556f0769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542588} {:answer/id #uuid "c98eda7f-7904-59d5-aed5-f095f55995d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542592} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f6bd32-8ed5-5aec-826d-0d30d73baf91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542594} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a59230-cdc0-59cf-8d49-a7d059d179cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2564c8e6-dd58-5512-bb79-d13a805cc112" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x$ along the line $y=2 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{3}(t)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{\\sqrt{5} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{3} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left((3)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\left(9-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{9 \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} {:answer/id #uuid "33be0b46-f243-554c-9d9e-964dc5eb30b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542564} {:answer/id #uuid "6c57458e-2426-55cb-b5d8-65aeae4d3130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542574} {:answer/id #uuid "c56967e6-dc2a-5dcc-9976-37549a83e6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542591} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e63c2b-a619-54eb-9772-8aa7f8b9dbf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020d5907-9b7b-5719-a1f1-4f44c5409284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d538b1dd-c792-572f-8c1f-8353d9802950" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bf9d63c-b3f4-5d38-83c5-c18e8598dd9c" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$ along the line $y=2 x + 1,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=2 t + 1.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(2 t + 1)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x \\left(y - 1\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{2}(2 t^{2})(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left((2)^{3}-(0)^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\left(8-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07329d51-3f5e-5e2e-8441-b2141aff317e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35 \\sqrt{37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542558} {:answer/id #uuid "224fc2fd-156e-5950-be40-d81695a7789b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542562} {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} {:answer/id #uuid "7105e2b6-33c5-596d-aaf1-e33906b7e9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542575} {:answer/id #uuid "f9544065-9a17-5e0c-a38b-236361697c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9065}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a9bc69-d00d-5b76-b39d-357b71605439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f81595a-0aec-5788-9a31-df107d898cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2eade040-7c90-5638-966c-718b41cfb998" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=5 x$ along the line $y=6 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 7.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=6 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,6 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 7\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(6 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(6)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(6)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{37}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{7}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{7}(5 t)(\\sqrt{37})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{7} \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left((7)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5 \\sqrt{37}}{2}\\left(49-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{245 \\sqrt{37}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e40f44d-615f-5865-a602-f53e98d24e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15 \\sqrt{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542561} {:answer/id #uuid "65d7c7ed-cf67-5d74-9d2d-7d230e621f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542572} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc6fca7-a70d-5ad4-b81d-03df2ea33f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542589} {:answer/id #uuid "cc301a85-6415-5f5a-a1ec-c9814b9c754e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0e57f6d-ff24-5498-8177-a14094d5bd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542586} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9afa8e-4db7-5a3a-a384-3177ceb89729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab95a8c-ac51-5396-919b-6a451ca13ac6" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=3 x$ along the line $y=4 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 5.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=4 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,4 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 5\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(4 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(4)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(4)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{5}(3 t)(\\sqrt{17})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17} t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left((5)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\left(25-0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{75 \\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b718af0-92d3-59c9-9229-69b2816bd3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542559} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} {:answer/id #uuid "5c916508-666a-5d4f-bc0e-83237918f953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{380}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542569} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd61603-c8b6-5e98-acdd-11a2306975c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542571} {:answer/id #uuid "9a892440-907b-5477-a7e0-478061c8676f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53bf3405-0c98-553b-9ce6-af3d6065c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542568} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c75bfe-126d-5238-b88a-6cc39af08281" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74138378-8762-53b8-ac46-3f0ace177732" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)$ along the line $y=3x+2,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,3t+2⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)j \\\\  & =i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{2}+(3t+2)={t}^{2}+3t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\,(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+2t){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{8}{3}+6+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{38 \\sqrt{10}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9920b7-0e21-5c1f-8111-86f228f479e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542580} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2807fa-9092-5089-8280-849eef5d1d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{285}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542581} {:answer/id #uuid "9e06ccd9-0287-543d-9f3b-377e3c903179" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(18446744073709551616 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542585} {:answer/id #uuid "bc780127-f10d-5f5e-8853-c61ea96f5041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3} + \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c2c9bda-2b1c-547d-a80e-eff23e39c06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542563} :answer-field/id #uuid "927562b5-720d-53ed-bf92-54fcb887de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e94a1c8-32a5-5aeb-82a3-988f3971786f" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{17}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{17} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({17}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(17 \\sqrt{17}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{34 \\sqrt{17}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b716c-5732-5534-84fd-923827a99c6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542566} {:answer/id #uuid "6a75de59-ff24-5015-82fd-549b0eabf5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542573} {:answer/id #uuid "77c7a369-2abc-53dd-b2aa-5a817fb5b5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542576} {:answer/id #uuid "871f3101-6660-58fb-8c35-50aad648b843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542579} {:answer/id #uuid "8d989555-4656-5801-a461-47d42c1dd876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77c7a369-2abc-53dd-b2aa-5a817fb5b5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72 \\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542576} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a0cf1d-2ab0-543e-9e64-7b0edf5e8453" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "969d5e52-a658-5dd3-9654-fc884ce1b67b" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=4 x$ along the line $y=5 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 6.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,5 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(5 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(5)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(5)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=4 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{6}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{6}(4 t)(\\sqrt{26})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[2 \\sqrt{26} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{6} \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left((6)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\sqrt{26}\\left(36-0\\right) \\\\ &=72 \\sqrt{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d630ca6-fd28-58ed-995a-8da968fd5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542560} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa57f44-2642-593c-a3e7-ef6c93fe59eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542570} {:answer/id #uuid "867db4e2-70dd-5f07-8dc4-522fdb1b0abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542578} {:answer/id #uuid "b4af4815-a8ff-5192-b441-27ae750e4043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d77a5-ca55-5835-beb8-b8509e3fef56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa2eae58-23f0-53d8-954b-616c559e82a9" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x$ along the line $y=3 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(3 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(3)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt})^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(2 t)(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left((4)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left(16-0\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5351d84c-4882-5054-a0c1-cbc0e962e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5351d84c-4882-5054-a0c1-cbc0e962e2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542864} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbb16c8-6341-55ff-b300-2282e814f399" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0054ec1e-580f-564e-8493-248291e1368b" :question/math-academy-id "q-281742" :question/problem "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,\\sqrt{3}),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/def95f29f6397b0f6eab20afb6327d8f3f99fdc52fbeac0b0c2af8b228d060a9.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18804b67-878e-56f2-a908-9dd72d5c6203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542701} {:answer/id #uuid "74910754-3a09-5360-8578-a6e630ecceac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542945} {:answer/id #uuid "846f084c-4d8e-5b3c-94e0-bd4bc2d3d3de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542995} {:answer/id #uuid "9eff8071-7a7a-58ad-9478-1815b1968cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543063} {:answer/id #uuid "b99e14c7-a03d-57b7-b791-a59cc36431e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18804b67-878e-56f2-a908-9dd72d5c6203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542701} :answer-field/id #uuid "92823537-f145-578d-8121-413772b2c835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15c5c76c-d78d-5592-bdec-a5d9824a4594" :question/math-academy-id "q-66765" :question/problem "Express ${log}_{2}⁡(7)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, the given expression can be rewritten as:\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(7)-{log}_{2}⁡(5)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{7}{5})\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fdf82a0-2583-54a3-bd7d-1ee3403c2774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(y)-{log}_{3}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542727} {:answer/id #uuid "31ee8031-ab23-5347-a954-5922df481593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\cdot {log}_{3}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542772} {:answer/id #uuid "7f80c10e-5297-5c3f-976a-b2a1b6bb66bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x)}{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542983} {:answer/id #uuid "b25d0d08-c661-51ef-9ff7-c14e5da09762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)+{log}_{3}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543104} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc6e0b1-d90c-5f5b-9ef7-e7b55fb39ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecc6e0b1-d90c-5f5b-9ef7-e7b55fb39ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543239} :answer-field/id #uuid "058dd5c5-0df5-5561-a280-d0460c220852" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20936b5f-864e-5ae6-90f1-dfba78a6f020" :question/math-academy-id "q-47257" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190687e3-a7f2-5a29-8dc7-a0bea5da56e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-2\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542705} {:answer/id #uuid "7581ab19-811f-5e47-b443-b607c614c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542948} {:answer/id #uuid "78a1a98e-88c2-5374-88b4-1257de2627c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542960} {:answer/id #uuid "ad34430e-a9d9-513c-8d4f-f1869c33c3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543095} {:answer/id #uuid "e87adda8-665d-5091-a708-f9408a979f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{log}⁡(y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7581ab19-811f-5e47-b443-b607c614c0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542948} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b9d0a5-0953-5666-a839-4b5670a6fac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c8cf324-472b-5836-8692-8d44663200a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1101" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bf6bafc-4476-5a9e-af7f-e7e2638c73cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bf6bafc-4476-5a9e-af7f-e7e2638c73cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542756} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cf19871-6dd2-593d-bf3f-f7ad27ac4fd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8e220e-5d02-535f-8203-20819203c421" :question/math-academy-id "q-281737" :question/problem "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/72/72cb94d6ed11f7502e7810d7d201ba71e594ed97a7a3eadd789732ca3dcb8968.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c14bd25-60b1-5db9-a5b5-db353df1d229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542757} {:answer/id #uuid "6a19ff45-4f16-58c5-a481-27c3e6c6c4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542925} {:answer/id #uuid "780d7c64-0c00-5d57-b7ce-66b2f26ef78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542957} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1f2a50-d198-5902-817a-d0db539435f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543053} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed9741b-2e78-58ae-b8cc-62e01e46a889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "780d7c64-0c00-5d57-b7ce-66b2f26ef78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542957} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd01a93c-d6e2-540b-8c0d-44c14937bd8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3044149e-5bfc-5de0-b490-60c442d8ed07" :question/math-academy-id "q-45533" :question/problem "What is the solution to  $2\\cdot {8}^{y}=\\frac{1}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by dividing both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{y} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {8}^{y}}{2} & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{2} \\\\ {8}^{y} & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{8}$ as ${8}^{-1}$ to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{y} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {8}^{y} & ={8}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(8)$, we equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{y} & ={8}^{-1} \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cece677-9803-52b0-b907-23768dbe2ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542667} {:answer/id #uuid "6929a7d0-50d3-5bd8-b005-5eaf6c8afc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542922} {:answer/id #uuid "6a89e1fb-1817-5ee7-bb66-dae6fa7f2260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542928} {:answer/id #uuid "ec14ff8e-fb9a-5ae2-9974-b0a7e6bb510d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{log}_{2}⁡(x)-2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543236} {:answer/id #uuid "fb088d0f-9bbb-51fc-8107-4545e4127621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)-2{log}_{2}⁡(y)+{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6929a7d0-50d3-5bd8-b005-5eaf6c8afc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542922} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2475f77-a3bc-521f-922f-608b63eb6022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34847574-efca-5670-8924-e70a3bcc31ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-66923" :question/problem "${log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z})={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z)={log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) & ={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{1/2})+{log}_{2}⁡({y}^{2})-{log}_{2}⁡(z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}{y}^{2}}{z})=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+2{log}_{2}⁡(y)-{log}_{2}⁡(z).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbd9de5-ffb6-5a92-af24-1a190f516d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542760} {:answer/id #uuid "42f17b6e-adee-5418-8a80-24659c92c3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-57.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542820} {:answer/id #uuid "48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-55.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542836} {:answer/id #uuid "a31bea97-b4ca-5c19-a543-03494bd16286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543071} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf9a326-b0aa-5098-aa51-d8aa62a227d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-51.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48337ab4-cd65-553e-b93c-57c20fa8194e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-55.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542836} :answer-field/id #uuid "689ba3cb-114a-56ff-8abb-81f20075f5f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47a49d65-e85d-5b9b-a8c6-e21bc37526ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-159947" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6.\n$$\n\n \nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and standard deviation $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=41.2,$ ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=182.6,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{10}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(182.6-2\\theta \\cdot 41.2+10{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-91.3)+41.2\\theta +(-5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-91.3)+41.2+(-5)=-55.1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08538244-0a29-5cc7-b86c-0a8d7eb92a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542656} {:answer/id #uuid "5d51face-45fc-5f92-aa4c-e940cfa9f9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542890} {:answer/id #uuid "69b0b1b4-2068-59f6-8caa-0232583e7632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542924} {:answer/id #uuid "bd225b51-2569-5aab-a898-96374e23410e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543135} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bfefa4-4a1a-5bbe-945c-432a0e867b73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69b0b1b4-2068-59f6-8caa-0232583e7632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542924} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6009af-c9fb-5d51-99cd-1ba483beaabc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49c1090f-0d14-5ccb-9c30-a8b9c4fffd87" :question/math-academy-id "q-79403" :question/problem "Express $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166addb8-c2f1-5d2d-9873-0a17afec2f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542695} {:answer/id #uuid "276974b1-5543-5e63-b8c1-928234d07545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542742} {:answer/id #uuid "61e1ae29-15b8-582a-aa4f-7bb4c3defafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{5}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542905} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5cb179-ffca-5fe5-89e3-4dc4c59454a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542973} {:answer/id #uuid "88f6512a-e755-51b7-8dea-3427afc1e864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c5cb179-ffca-5fe5-89e3-4dc4c59454a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542973} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee605d5-badf-5ba4-afaa-ea809cbe4caf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d345e50-4921-588f-ae74-039f20e6e137" :question/math-academy-id "q-100242" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{ex}{5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ex)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542638} {:answer/id #uuid "32606e7b-b40b-50e3-a1d1-5d1910806be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "370" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542774} {:answer/id #uuid "78fc0312-8634-566a-bd85-e6da768f8465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542961} {:answer/id #uuid "90057f88-a3e0-5ac7-8640-ecf0b41f4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543016} {:answer/id #uuid "be134ae9-dcf1-5a55-b9fd-d851e56132fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "390" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542638} :answer-field/id #uuid "e903e461-23e0-5fcd-b90c-a7d9090e44fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" :question/math-academy-id "q-121513" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={480}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{480}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={160}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={80}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={320}^{∘}$ and $x={400}^{∘}.$ We reject the solution ${400}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={80}^{∘},\\,{160}^{∘},\\,{320}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={80}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={320}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\text{ }+{x}_{3}$ is $80+320=400.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f7bf1e4-9398-55b3-bd2b-8515162867e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542897} {:answer/id #uuid "94e0befa-4705-51e9-9630-de948e992209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543030} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ba97b2-62e5-5ad9-99a5-29d92dd18008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543086} {:answer/id #uuid "e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543222} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a279b-4104-5ca5-8808-72ea4b362832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5189590-8a2a-5040-af84-68d2107b8f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543222} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c3ae4e-5559-5bc7-8d50-9bcf40432395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c4c55fe-2946-5795-a8c6-76c349270710" :question/math-academy-id "q-159960" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,{1}^{2}),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. The log-likelihood function of the sample $0.5,\\,-0.8,\\,-1.3$ is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+a+b\\theta +c{\\theta }^{2}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x,\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNext, we write down the log-density function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡f(x,\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{(x-\\theta )^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.5+(-0.8)+(-1.3) \\\\  & =-1.6 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}+(-0.8)^{2}+(-1.3)^{2} \\\\  & =2.58\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=3.$ Therefore,  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(2.58-2\\theta \\cdot (-1.6)+3{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =-1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )+(-1.29)+(-1.6)\\theta +(-1.5){\\theta }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=-1.29+(-1.6)+(-1.5)=-4.39.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02678531-9c49-5d43-ac23-ff708b70c36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542643} {:answer/id #uuid "61ecb54f-4cf4-55d8-87f1-9831a5a2ebfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542908} {:answer/id #uuid "998509c8-dbe2-5773-9816-4eebd147b0d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543041} {:answer/id #uuid "cf7c9130-467b-5bbc-adb8-bda779f897f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543176} {:answer/id #uuid "f056c49a-e227-56b9-82af-8dff44909eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf7c9130-467b-5bbc-adb8-bda779f897f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543176} :answer-field/id #uuid "73fea5df-076e-5c43-b1fe-9e9657547c19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fff1012-20ae-54c4-8327-06d77bbed9eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121522" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{-\\frac{9}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$  \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/999a34650676174083aa479669eecca0c837dc8e5f1397a81a8949d8f9dc9de1.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e71254c-e0c3-52aa-adb5-692437b5a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542670} {:answer/id #uuid "17c609b3-a070-57e3-b76f-89aea6bb093e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542700} {:answer/id #uuid "1b77aee6-038f-5fb6-8e37-86b0bbba4aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542711} {:answer/id #uuid "a65b53c3-6b83-58ec-ab00-d0e3e1f10487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543076} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ba5cbd-77f0-51e1-853b-981e94517aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0ba5cbd-77f0-51e1-853b-981e94517aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543257} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c89b6fc-2543-5375-ab12-8c5c3b4ded4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543396} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aa47990-58f3-5096-8b77-8c4ac5b32a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542846} {:answer/id #uuid "809028e1-3f32-54af-9337-604142d70915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542985} {:answer/id #uuid "9c089454-6485-5a34-8c18-f408f079ca03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543049} {:answer/id #uuid "b1218580-144b-5528-875c-a87a479b27f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543102} {:answer/id #uuid "bd81cd0e-c825-53fe-8a34-c3d587ddcf8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543136} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d5e092-eb0f-57a1-9f9e-03c7c474790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aa47990-58f3-5096-8b77-8c4ac5b32a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542846} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0c0ee2-17d3-59de-a732-734f1a510b76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "648c7fb6-34ca-53e3-ba2b-a6cf79213062" :question/math-academy-id "q-338913" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+49{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 7\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\lambda x)+7B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+7B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-7A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\lambda )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n14\\lambda =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{14}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2284e9-4930-533e-bba1-f292b232ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542708} {:answer/id #uuid "2b33e9f2-06d0-52b1-a7b7-b07f7a75beeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(24)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542754} {:answer/id #uuid "67971526-2da3-573b-889f-1f6897fcc514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542919} {:answer/id #uuid "b676aa76-e411-5e72-8f33-6c2b1d560616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543113} {:answer/id #uuid "beb71651-a15a-5287-b002-925ef99ff434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a2284e9-4930-533e-bba1-f292b232ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542708} :answer-field/id #uuid "575fce98-8f44-5a96-9baa-f92440382dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "692975c7-d71f-5bff-afaa-3fff34d21e62" :question/math-academy-id "q-99953" :question/problem "Express $6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for natural logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(b)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17) & =6(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{68}{17}) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(68)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3def5748-f5f8-53b5-b6ea-8e14945dfed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{9}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{z}{9}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542809} {:answer/id #uuid "62a351de-d274-575d-b200-5387096347ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{z}{2}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542910} {:answer/id #uuid "93a145b7-2cd7-59b3-8ff3-26e918720c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{9}-z)(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{z}{9}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2085ff-c8a6-5372-acca-6f59e8fca692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}-z)(\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543132} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b9f62d-cf08-5a64-aeb7-1cab2092352e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543260} :answer-field/id #uuid "b77b7c3f-41cf-5d84-b905-19ea7c30c507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "733ab756-eb23-5365-83ae-dfce071139ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-3600" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}+{b}^{3}=(a+b)({a}^{2}-ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write our expression as a sum of cubes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+{z}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the sum of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{27}+{z}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+{z}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}+z)({(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}+z)(\\frac{1}{9}-\\frac{z}{3}+{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0269a173-54ba-5ccf-a6e0-995965a165aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542644} {:answer/id #uuid "4e543ebb-1b6e-57d7-a4cc-de52b91accec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13)-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542852} {:answer/id #uuid "71ac5314-1262-522d-bc00-0042ccd70e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542940} {:answer/id #uuid "ba338d13-209f-57cf-bc5e-38caaf642b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543127} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f1b615-0943-5954-af8a-c55ae24c583b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba338d13-209f-57cf-bc5e-38caaf642b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543127} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f4d0f6-7f8a-58c2-8e17-d618255d36d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a291781-59c8-57ab-aafe-d8f0841ec267" :question/math-academy-id "q-47198" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient quotient and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{z}^{6}}{13}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542653} {:answer/id #uuid "42f98072-159d-5073-bea3-fc772168038c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542821} {:answer/id #uuid "4903b99d-0682-5a79-8fb4-e2b4b7c0f41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{t}+5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542839} {:answer/id #uuid "50b5e943-9ca0-5aed-9175-6b9302edbbc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{t}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542860} {:answer/id #uuid "ee53056d-d85d-50b2-b983-34612d0e6c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a88805-1323-5ff5-8f48-42543b26f532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542653} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b298728-bbf2-5c29-9156-fbe40121077d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82176286-a684-5858-a421-cc4a4b4187bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-69640" :question/problem "Factor $\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of cubes formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}-{b}^{3}=(a-b)({a}^{2}+ab+{b}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $8.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $8,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000=8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}-125)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of cubes:\n\n$$\n8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}-125)=8({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{3}-{5}^{3})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression in parentheses using the difference of cubes formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{t}^{6}}-1000 & =8({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{3}-{5}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)({(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 5+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-5)(\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{5}{{t}^{2}}+25)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "986de02b-222a-5a6d-891b-6e5b891ef90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543037} {:answer/id #uuid "9eef2f87-e855-5d53-80f4-202dbca3fd5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543061} {:answer/id #uuid "a677f03a-acb9-5439-be1f-b1eb7dee2b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543078} {:answer/id #uuid "b67ada9d-787b-5291-af4f-7eef69f5678d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543114} {:answer/id #uuid "e80ede49-5f0c-5bd8-a8bd-3022152fe7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b67ada9d-787b-5291-af4f-7eef69f5678d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd04507-0815-5cf4-b8b9-c2671207a55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "841f3191-5c7e-5e47-b725-127033cb9a73" :question/math-academy-id "q-79453" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, then ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nNote that ${log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})$ can be rewritten using the product and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2})+{log}_{5}⁡({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${log}_{5}⁡(x)=3$ and ${log}_{5}⁡(y)=2$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2{log}_{5}⁡(x)+3{log}_{5}⁡(y)=2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2=12.\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡({x}^{2}{y}^{3})=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170013cd-1ce2-5957-923e-754a4df4bd3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542699} {:answer/id #uuid "390b5693-f1ca-5a29-a8de-7aacb1098e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542790} {:answer/id #uuid "3c27c541-3a63-5fc4-ab19-bafc688b3c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542803} {:answer/id #uuid "6173b34d-e283-50ae-9765-c9f0bdcdfe52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)-{log}_{3}⁡(x)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542904} {:answer/id #uuid "8e12ed65-2a4a-5809-a1a7-871496b64eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)-{log}_{3}⁡(x)-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c27c541-3a63-5fc4-ab19-bafc688b3c69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542803} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f77377e-ee2a-59ef-bb46-445ff072d429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b91269e-ac3e-5858-87ab-575f9e7b0df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-66781" :question/problem "${log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27})=$" :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the product rule for logarithms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{27}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(5x)-{log}_{3}⁡(27) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{3}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(5)+{log}_{3}⁡(x)-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
